# rmgr-policy.table — TSR-RMGR-001 per-resource-manager boot policy.
#
# Phase 1 (study-level, dev-twin policy). Maps each manifested resource
# manager to the action the bring-up verifier takes when that RM fails to
# arm. '#' comments and blank lines ignored.
#
# Field 1: rmgr token (must match an entry in rmgr.manifest)
# Field 2: policy, one of:
#            block    -> arm failure BLOCKS bring-up (exit non-zero)
#            degrade  -> arm failure ANNUNCIATED, bring-up continues (flag set)
#            ignore   -> arm failure tolerated by explicit reviewed exception
#
# Per the FuSa §6 dev-twin policy (TSR-RMGR-001 OQ-A1 disposition):
# annunciate-and-allow for the dev twin only; the SAME condition is "block"
# on any leg that asserts a safety claim. The default for an RM not listed
# here is "block" (fail-loud). Each "ignore"/"degrade" below is an explicit,
# reviewed dev-twin exception, NOT a silent allowance.
#
# Networking is "degrade": the host io-sock stack does not initialise on this
# qemu-virt build (NF-9 / TSR-NET-001), the net path is inert by design, and
# the guest is launched no-network on purpose. Recorded loud, allowed for the
# dev twin. sshd is "degrade" because TCR-ENT-001 fail-secure may itself
# withhold sshd (entropy gate) — that is a correct outcome, not a bring-up
# block. slogger2/PCI/fsevmgr are "block": no diagnostics / no storage / no
# bus = no trustworthy bring-up.

slogger2      block
PCI Services  block
fsevmgr       block
Networking    degrade
sshd          degrade
